I'm looking at the new notebook and data files to understand what's happening with the lateral domain alignment. The user is asking a crucial question about whether the domains were already aligned before the trajectory lithography, or if the normal scan is what's causing the alignment. With the sharper diamond probe, the LDART images before any lithography show what appears to be aligned lateral domains, which raises two possibilities: either the film genuinely has pre-existing aligned in-plane domains in this region, or the imaging process itself is affecting the alignment.

The second possibility is actually quite plausible and important. A diamond probe is more conductive, which means at the same drive amplitude the voltage drop across the tip is lower, so more of the applied voltage appears across the film. Combined with the sharper geometry concentrating the electric field, the imaging drive voltage could potentially exceed the coercive field locally. The coercive window is around 2.5-3 V while the DART drive was only 0.30-0.50 V, which seems well below threshold. But accounting for the tip radius difference—a diamond probe might be 10-20 nm versus a worn metal-coated tip at 50-100 nm—the field concentration could be 3-5× higher. Running the numbers, 0.5 V at a 20 nm tip radius gives roughly 25 MV/m, while the coercive field for a 200 nm film would be around 12.5 MV/m. So yes, the imaging drive alone could exceed the coercive field with a sharp diamond tip, and this is a testable hypothesis.

To distinguish whether the ordering is pre-existing or created by the scan itself, I can run several experiments: scan the same area repeatedly and watch if the order increases with each pass; compare the first scan line to the last within a single frame to see if later-scanned regions are more ordered; reduce the drive voltage on a fresh area and compare the results;

